<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_merge.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-merge">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-merge">
  <primary>MERGE</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>MERGE</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>MERGE</refname>
  <refpurpose>match a pattern if it exists, create it if it does not</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
MERGE <replaceable class="parameter">pattern</replaceable>
    [ ON { CREATE | MATCH } SET <replaceable class="parameter">set_item</replaceable> [, <replaceable class="parameter">set_item</replaceable> ] ... ] ...
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-merge-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>MERGE</command> is one clause doing the work of a <xref linkend="cypher-match"/>
   and a <xref linkend="cypher-create"/>. It looks for its pattern. If the pattern is there it
   behaves like a <command>MATCH</command>, and if it is not it behaves like a
   <command>CREATE</command>. Either way the pattern's variables end up bound, so the rest of
   the query need not know which happened.
  </para>

  <para>
   One rule decides everything else. The clause considers the pattern as a whole. If the entire
   pattern matches, nothing is written. If any part of it is missing, the entire pattern is
   created, including the parts that already exist elsewhere in the graph. Merging
   <literal>(a)-[:e]-&gt;(b)</literal> when both vertices exist but the edge does not creates a
   second <literal>a</literal> and a second <literal>b</literal> as well as the edge.
  </para>

  <para>
   So merge one element at a time when you are not sure what is already there. Use a
   <command>MERGE</command> per vertex, then a <command>MERGE</command> for the edge between
   the variables they bound. Written that way, <command>MERGE</command> is idempotent. One
   clause takes one pattern, so several patterns need several clauses.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>MERGE</command> runs once per incoming row. It may lead a query, follow another
   clause, and end one.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-merge-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">pattern</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      One path pattern in <xref linkend="cypher-match"/> syntax, described in
      <xref linkend="cypher-path"/>. Every property in it is both a match condition and, on
      create, a property to set.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>ON CREATE SET</literal></term>
    <term><literal>ON MATCH SET</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <literal>ON CREATE SET</literal> applies its assignments only when the pattern was
      created, <literal>ON MATCH SET</literal> only when it was found. Both may be given, in
      either order.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">set_item</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      One assignment in <xref linkend="cypher-set"/> syntax:
      <literal>variable.key = expression</literal>, <literal>variable = map</literal> or
      <literal>variable += map</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-merge-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   The first run creates:
<programlisting>
MERGE (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'}) RETURN m.title AS film, m.year AS year;

  film   | year
---------+------
 "Tenet" |
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   Only <literal>title</literal> was in the pattern, so <literal>year</literal> is unset. A
   property in the pattern is part of what is matched, so this clause matches only a
   <literal>movie</literal> whose <literal>title</literal> is exactly
   <literal>Tenet</literal>. The second run finds the same vertex and writes nothing:
<programlisting>
MERGE (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'}) RETURN m.title AS film, m.year AS year;

  film   | year
---------+------
 "Tenet" |
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The two branches can set different properties. Start from nothing:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'}) DETACH DELETE m;

UPDATE 1
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
MERGE (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
ON CREATE SET m.year = 2020, m.source = 'created'
ON MATCH SET m.source = 'matched'
RETURN m.title AS film, m.year AS year, m.source AS source;

  film   | year |  source
---------+------+-----------
 "Tenet" | 2020 | "created"
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   The identical statement run a second time takes the other branch:
<programlisting>
MERGE (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
ON CREATE SET m.year = 2020, m.source = 'created'
ON MATCH SET m.source = 'matched'
RETURN m.title AS film, m.year AS year, m.source AS source;

  film   | year |  source
---------+------+-----------
 "Tenet" | 2020 | "matched"
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   <literal>year</literal> keeps the value the create branch gave it, because
   <literal>ON MATCH SET</literal> did not touch it.
  </para>

  <para>
   There is one Christopher Nolan, and he has not directed <literal>Tenet</literal>:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'}) DETACH DELETE m;
MATCH (p:person {name: 'Christopher Nolan'}) RETURN count(*) AS nolans;

UPDATE 1
 nolans
--------
 1
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   Merging the whole path finds no match, because the edge is missing, so it creates the whole
   path:
<programlisting>
MERGE (d:person {name: 'Christopher Nolan'})-[:directed]-&gt;(m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
RETURN d.name AS director, m.title AS film;

      director       |  film
---------------------+---------
 "Christopher Nolan" | "Tenet"
(1 row)
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person {name: 'Christopher Nolan'}) RETURN count(*) AS nolans;

 nolans
--------
 2
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   The second Christopher Nolan has no <literal>born</literal> property and none of the
   original's edges. This is the mistake <command>MERGE</command> invites.
  </para>

  <para>
   Undo that, then merge each element separately:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person {name: 'Christopher Nolan'})
WHERE NOT EXISTS { MATCH (p)-[:directed]-&gt;(:movie {title: 'Inception'}) }
DETACH DELETE p;
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'}) DETACH DELETE m;

UPDATE 2
UPDATE 1
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
MERGE (d:person {name: 'Christopher Nolan'})
MERGE (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
MERGE (d)-[:directed]-&gt;(m)
RETURN d.name AS director, m.title AS film;

      director       |  film
---------------------+---------
 "Christopher Nolan" | "Tenet"
(1 row)
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person {name: 'Christopher Nolan'}) RETURN count(*) AS nolans;

 nolans
--------
 1
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   The first <command>MERGE</command> matched the existing person, the second created the film,
   the third created just the edge. Running the same three clauses again changes nothing.
  </para>

  <para>
   More than one pattern is refused by the grammar:
<programlisting>
MERGE (a:movie {title: 'A'}), (b:movie {title: 'B'});

ERROR:  syntax error at or near ","
LINE 1: MERGE (a:movie {title: 'A'}), (b:movie {title: 'B'});
                                    ^
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-merge-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   A unique constraint turns the duplication risk into a clean failure rather than silent data
   corruption. Remove the merged film first:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'}) DETACH DELETE m;

UPDATE 2
</programlisting>
   With <literal>name</literal> unique on <literal>person</literal>, the merge of the whole
   path cannot create its second Nolan:
<programlisting>
CREATE CONSTRAINT unique_person_name ON person ASSERT name IS UNIQUE;
MERGE (d:person {name: 'Christopher Nolan'})-[:directed]-&gt;(m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
RETURN d.name AS director, m.title AS film;

CREATE CONSTRAINT
ERROR:  conflicting key value violates exclusion constraint "unique_person_name"
DETAIL:  Key ((properties.'name'))=("Christopher Nolan") conflicts with existing key ((properties.'name'))=("Christopher Nolan").
</programlisting>
   Nothing is created, and the transaction reports the reason.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-merge-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   ISO/IEC 39075:2024 has no <command>MERGE</command>. Its data changing statements are the
   insert statement of section 13.2, the set statement of 13.3, the remove statement of 13.4
   and the delete statement of 13.5. The standard way to write it is an <literal>EXISTS</literal>
   test, or a <xref linkend="cypher-call"/> subquery, followed by <xref linkend="cypher-insert"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>MERGE</command> comes from openCypher, and AgensGraph keeps the openCypher
   semantics: the pattern is considered as a whole, and both branches take the same assignment
   list as <xref linkend="cypher-set"/>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

